# React Real-time — hooks & Redux bridge

## Subscription hooks
```ts
// good — singleton owns the socket; component subscribes via hook
const { phase, balance } = useGameTick(gameId);

// bad
useEffect(() => { const ws = new WebSocket("wss://…"); /* … */ }, []);
```
- Hook returns a snapshot; unsubscribes on unmount.
- Multiple subscribers to the same channel share one socket.
- Hooks live in `packages/ws-client/src/hooks.ts` (shared) or feature-local `ws/use<Thing>.ts`.

## Redux bridge (recommended)
- Middleware inside `packages/ws-client` decodes frames and dispatches typed actions.
- Components read derived state via selectors. Outbound: dispatch a `sendFrame` action.

## `useSyncExternalStore` (when not using Redux)
```ts
export function useChannel<T>(name: string, decoder: (frame: unknown) => T): T | null {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    (cb) => channelRegistry.subscribe(name, cb),
    () => channelRegistry.snapshot<T>(name),
    () => null,
  );
}
```
`getServerSnapshot` returns null (or safe default). Registry is module-level singleton; tests inject a fake.

## Render rate
- Hook output ≤ ~30 changes/s for non-game UIs (60 for canvases).
- `requestAnimationFrame` batching in the bridge or hook adapter.

## Testing
- `renderWithProviders` with WS bridge wired to in-memory fake.
- Push frames synchronously and `await screen.findByText(...)`.
- Cover: connect / disconnect-with-banner / malformed-ignored / reconnect-converges.
